{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310838,"nonce":"ABauHlivWYBuXPeQHe5/1/BZ8VE=","title":"JIT does not auto-vectorise Haxe-side numeric loops; all SIMD wins on Rayzor come from Rust runtime kernels reached via FFI, not from ...","message":"Do not write a scalar Haxe loop and expect the Rayzor JIT to turn it into NEON. It will not. Every f32x4 / int8x16 op in a running llama-chat binary lives in Rust runtime kernels and is reached via a `blr` from Haxe-emitted code. The Haxe loop bodies themselves emit pure scalar AArch64 (ldr/str/fmul/fadd) with zero `*.4s` / `*.16b` instructions.\n\nIf you want SIMD on a hot path, the answer is \"call a Rust kernel\" or \"fuse the FFI calls\", not \"rewrite the loop more tightly in Haxe\".\n\n\nDisassembled the actual JIT'd code of the four hottest Haxe-side numeric functions in a running llama-chat session:\n\n- `nue.layers.RMSNorm.forward`\n- `nue.layers.KVCache.append`\n- `nue.layers.Linear.forward`\n- `nue.layers.GQAttention.forward`\n\nResult: **zero NEON instructions** across all four. Inner loops are scalar `ldr s0, [...]` / `fmul s0, s0, s1` / `str s0, [...]`.\n\nThe same binary contains **747 NEON ops total**. Every single one of them is inside Rust runtime kernels (`runtime/src/tensor_simd.rs`, `runtime/src/quant.rs`, etc.) reached from Haxe via `blr` into `haxe_*` extern shims. Confirmed by symbol-resolving each `blr` target back to a Rust function.\n\nSo vectorisation on Rayzor today is a property of *which extern you call*, not of how the JIT compiles your loop.\n\n\n\n- `src/main.rs:1132` hard-pins the optimisation level at `OptLevel::O0` for the JIT path (see the `OptimizationConfig` construction site).\n- `compiler/src/ir/optimization.rs:1904` only adds `LoopVectorizationPass` to the pipeline when the level is `O3`.\n- Cranelift itself has no loop autovectoriser. (cf. `bytecodealliance/wasmtime#7906` — cranelift only does instruction-level SIMD when the input MIR already contains SIMD ops; it never widens `f32` loop bodies on its own.)\n\nNet: even if we wanted JIT autovec, the pass that would produce it is statically excluded from the running pipeline. Flipping `O0`→`O3` is not safe today (the rest of the O3 pipeline isn't proven on the cross-context BLADE path) and would still only buy us cause (2).\n\n\n- `compiler/src/ir/vectorization.rs:693-704` rejects loops whose trip count is not a compile-time constant. Every interesting loop in nue is `for i in 0...n` where `n` is `seqLen`, `hiddenDim`, `numHeads`, etc. — all runtime values. They are skipped by construction.\n- Even if we relaxed that, the hottest loops have nothing to widen. E.g. `nue/layers/GQAttention.hx:153` is:\n\n  ```haxe\n  for (h in 0...numQHeads) {\n    var qHead = q.slice(...);          // FFI\n    var kHead = k.slice(...);          // FFI\n    var scores = qHead.bmm(kHeadT);    // FFI\n    scores.softmaxInPlace();           // FFI\n    out.copyFrom(scores.bmm(vHead));   // FFI\n  }\n  ```\n\n  The body is a sequence of `blr` calls into Rust kernels. There is no arithmetic IR in the loop body for a vectoriser to widen — the arithmetic is already inside the called kernel, already vectorised.\n\n\nProfile of Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M on Rayzor: \u003e90 % of wall-time is inside vectorised Rust kernels (Q4_K_M dequant, qmatmul, RoPE, softmax). The Haxe-side glue (loop dispatch, slicing, control flow) is single-digit %.\n\nSo even a hypothetical perfect JIT autovectoriser that widened every remaining scalar Haxe loop perfectly would buy **2–8 % end-to-end wall-time on Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M.** It is not where the time is.\n\n\n- `runtime/src/tensor_simd.rs` — NEON FMA paths for matmul, bmm, elementwise, reductions.\n- `runtime/src/quant.rs` — SDOT / UDOT for Q4_K_M / Q6_K / INT8 dot products, NEON-block dequant.\n\nThese are the 747 NEON ops. They are called from Haxe via the `haxe_*` extern shims.\n\n\n`rayzor.SIMD4f` is a real, working SIMD type with full `@:op` lowering. It is implemented at:\n\n- `compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs:3668-3726` — native f32x4 add/sub/mul/div/fma + horizontal sum, etc.\n- `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs:2835-3015` — at f32x4 parity for WASM backend.\n\nIt works. It is not used. Zero call sites in `nue/`. We could rewrite `RMSNorm.forward` etc. using `SIMD4f` and the JIT would emit `fmla v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s` — but per the Amdahl bound above, that's a 2–8 % top-line win for substantial Haxe-side churn.\n\n\nNot JIT autovec. Two higher-leverage moves:\n\n1. **FFI fusion** — collapse adjacent `blr`-only sequences (e.g. `slice → bmm → softmaxInPlace → bmm → copyFrom` in GQAttention) into a single fused kernel call. Removes call overhead and lets the Rust side keep tensors resident in registers / L1 across the fused op. See [[project-qmatmul-threaded]] for the existing precedent.\n2. **The pool** — qmatmul thread pool work continues to be the dominant lever. See [[project-qmatmul-threaded]].\n\nIf we ever do want JIT autovec it is a long road: relax the constant-trip-count guard, prove the rest of the O3 pipeline on the cross-context BLADE path, and audit trap-stub cascade behaviour ([[bugs-trap-stub-cascade]]) under widened IR. Worth at most ~8 % at the end of it.\n\n\n- [[bugs-trap-stub-cascade]] — any change to the optimisation pipeline must respect the silent-stub cascade behaviour; surface failures with `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`.\n- [[project-qmatmul-threaded]] — the actual perf lever for llama-chat today.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_jit_no_autovectorisation` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}